# Advanced Computer Science
> Data structures, algorithms, and systems
@languageCode: en
// Swiq — https://swiq.jiun.dev/set/seed.en.exam-computer.cs-advanced/

Binary search finds a value in a sorted array in O(log n) time. | O | Binary search halves the search range each step, giving O(log n) time.
A stack is a Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) data structure. | O | In a stack, the most recently added element is removed first (LIFO).
A queue is a First-In-First-Out (FIFO) data structure. | O | In a queue, the first element added is the first removed (FIFO).
In a typical modern binary digital computer, each bit can represent any one of the decimal digits 0 through 9. | X | A bit has two possible values, 0 and 1. Multiple bits can encode decimal digits and other data.
One byte consists of 8 bits. | O | A byte is made up of 8 bits.
An ahead-of-time native compiler translates source code into machine code before that code is executed. | O | Ahead-of-time native compilation produces machine code before execution. Other compilers can produce intermediate code or compile during execution, as in JIT compilation.
In Java, the first element of a nonempty array has index 1. | X | Java array indices start at 0. An array of length n has indices 0 through n−1.
A hash table can offer average-case O(1) time for insertion and lookup. | O | With good hashing and collision handling, hash tables average O(1) operations.
Which function grows fastest as positive input size n tends to infinity? | 1)n² 2)n 3)log₂ n 4)1 | 1 | Among these functions, n² grows fastest for large n. Big-O notation describes an upper bound, which need not be tight.
Which data structure is best suited for Last-In-First-Out behavior? | 1)Graph 2)Stack 3)Queue 4)Array | 2 | A stack provides LIFO behavior; a queue is FIFO.
Q: What is the decimal value of the binary number 1010? (digits only) | A: 10;ten | 1010 in binary equals 8 + 0 + 2 + 0 = 10 in decimal.
Q: What do we call a flaw or error in a computer program, the removal of which is called debugging? | A: bug;a bug;software bug | A bug is an error in a program; fixing bugs is called debugging.
Standard top-down merge sort has worst-case O(n log n) time when comparisons and element moves take constant time. | O | This version splits the sequence into roughly equal halves, recursively sorts them, and merges each pair in linear time. The resulting worst-case time is O(n log n).
Standard quicksort that always chooses the first element as pivot has worst-case O(n log n) time for distinct elements. | X | With this pivot rule, an already sorted input repeatedly produces an empty partition and a partition of size n−1. With constant-time comparisons, the worst-case time is O(n²), not O(n log n).
Which binary-tree traversal visits the root, recursively traverses the entire left subtree, and then recursively traverses the entire right subtree? | 1)Level-order 2)In-order 3)Pre-order 4)Post-order | 3 | Pre-order follows root, left subtree, then right subtree recursively. Level-order instead visits nodes by depth.
A balanced binary search tree supports search, insertion, and deletion in O(log n) time. | O | Balanced BSTs like AVL or red-black trees keep their height at O(log n), so core operations run in O(log n).
DNS translates IP addresses into MAC addresses. | X | DNS resolves domain names to information such as IP addresses. On IPv4 Ethernet networks, ARP resolves a neighbor’s IPv4 address to a MAC address; IPv6 uses Neighbor Discovery for link-layer address resolution.
Q: What is the hexadecimal number FF in decimal? (digits only) | A: 255;two hundred fifty-five;two hundred and fifty-five | FF in hexadecimal is 15×16 + 15 = 255, the largest value of an unsigned 8-bit byte.
TCP provides reliable, ordered delivery of data, while UDP does not guarantee delivery. | O | TCP uses acknowledgments and retransmission to provide a reliable, ordered byte stream. UDP itself does not guarantee delivery or ordering; actual performance depends on the application and network.
The DRAM commonly used as main memory in personal computers retains its contents without power. | X | DRAM is volatile and needs power to retain data. Non-volatile storage such as an SSD retains data without power.
In the OSI model, which layer is responsible for routing packets between networks? | 1)Application layer 2)Physical layer 3)Transport layer 4)Network layer | 4 | The network layer (layer 3) handles logical addressing and routing of packets across networks; IP operates at this layer.
A deadlock can occur when processes each hold a resource while waiting for a resource held by another. | O | Deadlock arises from circular waiting: each process holds something another needs, so none can proceed.
In Big-O notation, O(1) means an algorithm performs exactly one operation. | X | O(1) means constant time — the number of operations does not grow with input size, not that only one operation occurs.
Q: What does the abbreviation CPU stand for? (three words) | A: central processing unit;the central processing unit | The Central Processing Unit executes instructions and is often called the 'brain' of the computer.
Given an existing head pointer and a preallocated node, insertion at a linked list’s head takes O(1) pointer updates, while front insertion into a packed array requires shifting its n elements. | O | Head insertion updates a fixed number of pointers. In a packed array whose first element occupies the start of its storage, front insertion shifts the existing elements, taking O(n) element moves.
Plain HTTP encrypts data in transit by default. | X | HTTP sends data in plaintext; HTTPS adds TLS encryption to protect data between browser and server.
Which data structure does breadth-first search (BFS) typically use to track the next nodes to visit? | 1)Queue 2)Stack 3)Heap 4)Tree | 1 | BFS explores level by level using a queue; depth-first search uses a stack or recursion instead.
A finite, connected, undirected graph with no cycles is called a tree. | O | In graph theory, a tree is a connected, undirected, acyclic graph. A finite tree with n vertices has n−1 edges.
The Windows operating system kernel runs in user mode. | X | Core Windows operating system components run in kernel mode, while ordinary application code runs in user mode.
Q: What is the name of the smallest unit of digital information, representing a 0 or a 1? | A: bit;a bit;binary digit | A bit (binary digit) is the smallest unit of information; eight bits make one byte.
Java’s int type represents 32-bit signed integers using two’s complement. | O | The Java Language Specification defines int as a 32-bit signed two’s-complement type. This does not mean every integer representation in every language uses that format.
Recursion always uses less memory than an equivalent iterative solution. | X | Recursion usually uses more memory because each call adds a stack frame; deep recursion can even overflow the call stack.
What does SQL stand for? | 1)System Quality Language 2)Structured Query Language 3)Standard Query Logic 4)Sequential Question Language | 2 | SQL, Structured Query Language, is the standard language for querying and managing relational databases.
In a relational database, a primary key uniquely identifies each row in a table. | O | A primary key is a column (or set of columns) whose values are unique and non-null, identifying each row exactly once.
Moore's law states that processor clock speed doubles every year. | X | Moore's law is the observation that the number of transistors on a chip doubles roughly every two years — it is about transistor density, not clock speed.
Q: What acronym names the non-volatile memory that stores firmware and keeps its contents without power? | A: rom;read only memory;read-only memory | ROM (Read-Only Memory) is non-volatile and traditionally holds firmware such as a computer's boot code.
